# Files that belong to the repository but not to a release download.
#
# `export-ignore` removes them from `git archive`, which is what release.yml
# builds the distributable tarball with - and also what GitHub's own
# "Source code" links produce, so the two stay consistent.
#
# Anything a run needs must NOT be listed here. release.yml asserts both
# directions: required files present, these absent.

# docs/ is generated and untracked, so it cannot reach an archive in any case; the rule
# stays as insurance. manual/ is what it is generated FROM, and is deliberately absent from
# this list: a downloaded release carries the whole manual as one readable file, so the
# documentation does not depend on the site being reachable.
docs/               export-ignore
.github/            export-ignore
mkdocs.yml          export-ignore
.gitignore          export-ignore
.gitattributes      export-ignore

# Development scratch. Listed ahead of being tracked: once these are in git they
# are kept out of every release download automatically, so tracking them costs a
# user nothing.
dev/                export-ignore
Project/            export-ignore

# The test suite and its committed fixture data. Kept in the repository so every change can
# be checked against a known dataset, kept out of releases because a user never runs it.
test/               export-ignore

# NO MODULE SHIPS INSIDE A RELEASE. A module is published on its own timetable and installed
# from the catalogue, and that is the whole point of the module system - a release that carried
# three of them would make those three privileged, installed by nobody, and impossible to
# replace with a newer version without first removing what the release provided.
#
# The whole directory: modules and the libraries they install alongside themselves. They are
# sources here, and published artifacts in modules/repo/, which the site serves. A release
# carries neither: a catalogue inside a tarball would be a second answer to what can be
# installed, frozen on the day the release was built.
modules/           export-ignore

# How the project is built rather than what it does: the agent's instruction file and the
# development notes. Tracked, and linked from the manual's Development section, but a release
# download carries the pipeline and the manual - the same rule dev/ follows.
.claude/            export-ignore
CLAUDE.md           export-ignore
